  "image_name": "68e9040c729a9eab9f9faa14f81138dd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how Antarctic ice volume changed through the Holocene and what likely drove those changes. Across three time slices—early, mid, and late Holocene—it maps evidence around Antarctica for ice loss or gain, along with changes in grounding line, ice loading, interior thickness, and margin thickness. Most symbols indicate loss or retreat in many coastal sectors during the early and mid Holocene, especially around the Antarctic Peninsula, Weddell Sea, Ross Sea, and parts of East Antarctica, although the amount and confidence vary by region. By the late Holocene, evidence appears more mixed and in some places weaker, suggesting a shift away from widespread retreat toward more regionally variable behavior.\n\nThe lower part distills the main controls for each period. Early Holocene change is linked chiefly to oceanic forcing, sea-level change, and bed topography, with atmospheric influence marked as uncertain. In the mid Holocene, oceanic change and sea level remain important, while glacial isostatic adjustment begins to matter. By the late Holocene, sea level drops out and the dominant controls seem to be glacial isostatic adjustment and atmospheric change, with oceanic influence shown more tentatively. Simple schematics illustrate these mechanisms, such as warm-water incursions, sea-level forcing, pinning points, crustal rebound, and climate-mode effects.",
